## The auditd rules the lab itself needs, whatever sensor settings are under
## test. "bluefire-lab run" loads them after the settings' own audit rules.
##
## The lab has to know which process belongs to which test: the marker commands
## around a test, the shell the executor starts for it, and what that shell
## starts. It reads that from the record auditd makes of every program start,
## which names the process, its parent and its audit session. These rules make
## sure such a record exists for every start, also under sensor settings that
## record few of them, such as a published Sysmon configuration or published
## audit rules.
##
## The kernel takes the key of a record from the first rule that matches, in
## the order the rules were loaded. So a record that a rule of the settings
## under test would also have made carries that rule's key, and counts as that
## sensor's telemetry. A record that carries the key below exists only because
## of these rules: the lab uses it to tie events to tests, never counts it as
## telemetry of a test, and never gives it to the rule engine.

## A backlog large enough for a burst, whatever the settings under test set.
-b 32768

## The file the lab writes until auditd has recorded the write.
-w /var/tmp/bluefire-lab-audit-probe -p wa -k bluefire-lab-instrument

## Every program start.
-a always,exit -F arch=b64 -S execve -S execveat -k bluefire-lab-instrument
-a always,exit -F arch=b32 -S execve -S execveat -k bluefire-lab-instrument
